 A human considered to resemble monkeys in some way, including:", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "(informal, sometimes offensive) A naughty or mischievous person, especially a child.", "tags": ["figuratively", "informal", "offensive", "sometimes"], "examples": ["Stop misbehaving, you cheeky little monkey!"], "quotes": [{"text": "\"Yes. He gets to Paris at seven in the morning. He promised to telephone the first thing.\"\n\"You expensive little monkey!\"\n\"Why?\"\n\"It's ten shillings for three minutes, or something like that, and you have to go to the G.P.O. or the Mansion House or some such place, I believe.\"", "reference": "1909, Algernon Blackwood, You May Telephone From Here:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) The person in the motorcycle sidecar in sidecar racing.", "tags": ["figuratively", "slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(derogatory) Synonym of idiot: a person of minimal intelligence.", "tags": ["derogatory", "figuratively"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["idiot"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(derogatory) Synonym of uggo: an unattractive person, especially one whose face supposedly resembles a monkey's.", "tags": ["derogatory", "figuratively"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["uggo"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang, derogatory) Synonym of puppet: a person dancing to another's tune, a person controlled or directed by another.", "tags": ["derogatory", "figuratively", "slang"], "examples": ["No, no, no, not you. I want to talk to the organ grinder, not the monkey."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["puppet"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang, usually derogatory) A menial employee who does a repetitive job supposedly requiring minimal intelligence.", "tags": ["derogatory", "figuratively", "slang", "usually"], "examples": ["code monkey", "grease monkey", "phone monkey"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang, highly derogatory, ethnic slur, offensive) A black person.", "tags": ["ethnic", "figuratively", "offensive", "slang", "slur"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Animalistic dehumanisation, 'simianisation' and the animalising of black bodies through metaphorically likening these individuals to 'baboons,' 'monkeys' or 'apes' reveals racialised animality discourse in South Africa.", "reference": "2022 October, René Cornish, Kieran Tranter, “Dismissals for Social Media Hate Speech in South Africa: Animalistic Dehumanisation and the Circulation of Racist Words and Images”, in Springer Nature Link:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(slang, vulgar, uncommon) A penis.", "tags": ["slang", "uncommon", "vulgar"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(historical) A small trading vessel of the sixteenth century.", "tags": ["historical"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang, nautical) The vessel in which a mess receives its full allowance of grog.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The weight of a pile driver or drop hammer.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Someone handed me a monkey of grog. I forced myself to sip it, not down it.", "reference": "2007, Broos Campbell, No Quarter, page 111:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A fluid consisting of hydrochloric acid and zinc, used in the process of soldering.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) Synonym of five hundred, especially (British) 500 pounds sterling or (US, dated) 500 dollars.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "[I] gave her a monkey — just half my last thou — and started to earn some more.", "reference": "1918, Aleister Crowley, Robbing Miss Horniman:"}, {"text": "A thousandth of a million squid or two monkeys\nOr a whole fifty scores", "reference": "2004, The Streets, “It Was Supposed to Be So Easy”, in A Grand Don’t Come for Free:"}], "synonyms": ["five hundred"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(blackjack) Synonym of face card.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["face card"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) A person's temper, said to be \"up\" when they are angry.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I was out rather late one night, when the foreman of my department, who owed me a grudge, abused me like a dog, and told me I might consider myself dismissed, and that I should be paid my wages in the morning. I don't know how I kept my hands off him, for my monkey was up; […]", "reference": "1864, Eneas Sweetland Dallas, Once a Week, volume 11, page 267:"}, {"text": "Reminded me of running up that hill by the Marne – or was it the Morin? – in 1914 when Kearey had got his face ripped open by a bullet and Hedley had got his monkey up [Lost his temper, Ed.] over the Huns killing 8 of my platoon.", "reference": "2019, John Hughes-Wilson, 1918 - Defeat into Victory: A Tommy Gunn Adventure, page 98:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) A drug habit; an addiction; a compulsion.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": ["Monkey: a habit, as in \"I have a monkey on my back.\" Usually used when one is sick from lack of drugs.", "Everybody's got something to hide except for me and my monkey."], "quotes": [{"text": "\"Man, I wasn't hooked, I was crucified. The monkey got so big he was carryin' me. […] When I hear a junkie tell me he wants to kick the habit but he just can't I know he lies even if he don't know he does. He wants to carry the monkey, he's punishin' hisself for somethin' 'n don't even know it. […] Then I got forty grains 'n went up to the room 'n went from monkey to nothin' in twenny-eight days 'n that's nine-ten years ago 'n the monkey's dead.\"\n \"The monkey's never dead, Fixer,\" Frankie told him knowingly.", "reference": "1949, Nelson Algren, The Man with The Golden Arm:"}, {"text": "I thought I would like to learn about the dope scene anyway, and the boy must have some character, you know, if he got the monkey off his back (as they used to say in our time) without outside help.", "reference": "1976 September, Saul Bellow, Humboldt’s Gift, New York, N.Y.: Avon Books, →ISBN, page 431:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(dance) A dance popularized by Major Lance in 1963, now usually only its upper-body dance move involving exaggerated drumming motions.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Do the Monkey, yeah,\nDo the Monkey, yeah,\nAh, twist them hips,\nLet your backbone slip,\nNow move your feet,\nGet on the beat..."], "quotes": [{"text": "C'mon everybody! C'mon in!\nBobby's goin' to show you how to do the swim.\nKinda like the monkey, kinda like the twist\nPretend you're in the water and you go like this.", "reference": "1964, “C'mon and Swim”, Thomas Coman and Sylvester Stewart (Sly Stone) (lyrics), performed by Bobby Freeman:"}, {"text": "Do the monkey, do the pony / Do the slop, do the boogaloo twist.", "reference": "1971, “Suicide Song”, in Loudon Wainwright III (lyrics), Album II, performed by Loudon Wainwright III:"}, {"text": "...Used to the do the monkey\nBut now it's not cool...", "reference": "1989, Gary Holt, The Toxic Waltz:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["monkey"], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈmʌŋki/", "tags": ["General American", "Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈmʊŋki/", "tags": ["Northern England"]}], "forms": [{"word": "monkeys", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "monkeying", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "monkeyed", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "monkeyed", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "monkied", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "monkied", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(intransitive, informal) To meddle; to mess (with).", "tags": ["informal", "intransitive"], "examples": ["Please don't monkey with the controls if you don't know what you're doing."], "quotes": [{"text": "“As an inventor,” Bob Mason suggested, “you're a howling success at shooting craps! […] Why monkey with weak imitations when you can come close to the original?”", "reference": "1920, Peter B. Kyne, chapter XII, in The Understanding Heart:"}], "synonyms": ["interfere", "fiddle"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To mimic; to ape.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "He winked at Liza, who monkeyed him, holding her own eye shut.", "reference": "2011, Elizabeth Mosier, The Playgroup, page 83:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/monkey", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}